==English==

===Etymology===
{{bor|en|fro|aquest}}, (French {{m|fr|acquêt}}), from {{der|en|LL.|-}}, {{der|en|VL.|*acquestum}}, {{m|la|*acquaesītum}}, for {{der|en|la|acquisītum}}, past participle (used substantively) of {{m|la|acquirere||to acquire}}. See {{m|en|acquire}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|rare}} [[acquisition|Acquisition]]; the thing gained.
#: {{rfquotek|Francis Bacon}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} property acquired by purchase, gift, or otherwise than by inheritance.
#: {{rfquotek|John Bouvier}}

====Usage notes====
Bouvier, 1856, [http://www.constitution.org/bouv/bouvier_a.htm] from which the Webster derives, uses the spelling ''[[acquets]]''.

===References===
* {{R:Webster 1913}}

